  "style_primary": "post-bop",
  "rationale": "Ellington's elegy for Billy Strayhorn — a consensus-core late-Ellington record. It won the Grammy Award for Best Large Jazz Ensemble Album in 1969 (S1); AllMusic rates it 5 stars (\"among his most profoundly felt and very finest recorded moments\") and The Penguin Guide to Jazz Recordings 4 stars (both tabulated at S1); Ellington biographer John Edward Hasse writes that \"rarely did the entire band play with such heartfelt passion, and the album is widely considered one of Ellington's best\" (S1); JazzTimes singles out the closing solo piano \"Lotus Blossom\" as its most celebrated moment (S1). The program is all Strayhorn — including \"Blood Count\", his last composition — played by the Ellington orchestra of Johnny Hodges, Cootie Williams, Paul Gonsalves, Harry Carney, Clark Terry and the rest of the 1967 band (S1, S2). A defining statement, not merely a memorial.",
