    "tier": "consensus_core",
    "priority": "strong",
    "case_for": "The album has unusually broad recognition: the dossier reports a 1969 Grammy, five stars from AllMusic, four from The Penguin Guide, and biographer John Edward Hasse’s description of it as one of Ellington’s best (S1). Its all-Strayhorn program, including the final composition “Blood Count” and Ellington’s solo “Lotus Blossom,” makes it a focused, emotionally powerful statement, while the presence of Johnny Hodges, Paul Gonsalves, Harry Carney, Cootie Williams, and others gives the session exceptional gravity (S1, S2).",
